(window.RW_DISPLAY = window.RW_DISPLAY || {})["test05_1B"] = [{"id":"cf9a3f34","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"The following text is adapted from Elizabeth von Arnim’s 1922 novel The Enchanted April. Mrs. Wilkins and her friend Rose are traveling in Italy. “’ I’m going to have one of these gorgeous oranges,” said Mrs. Wilkins, staying where she was and reaching across to a black bowl piled with them. “Rose, how can you resist them. Look — —” have this one. Do have this beauty And she held out a big one. As used in the text,","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The following text is adapted from Elizabeth von Arnim’s 1922 novel The Enchanted April. Mrs. Wilkins and her friend Rose are traveling in Italy.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"“I’m going to have one of these gorgeous oranges,” said Mrs. Wilkins, staying where she was and reaching across to a black bowl","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"piled with them. “Rose, how can you resist them. Look—have this one. Do have this beauty—” And she held out a big one.","indent":2}],"question":"As used in the text, what does the phrase “reaching across to” most nearly mean?","choices":["Joining with","Gaining on","Stretching toward","Arriving at"],"underline":"reaching across to","underline_segments":["reaching across to"]},{"id":"0d402146","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"US traffic signals didn’t always contain the familiar three lights (red, yellow, and green). Traffic lights only ______ red and green lights until the three-light traffic signal was developed in the 1920s.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"US traffic signals didn’t always contain the familiar three lights (red, yellow, and green). Traffic lights only ______ red and green lights until the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"three-light traffic signal was developed in the 1920s.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["avoided","featured","appreciated","disregarded"]},{"id":"bce4d106","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"Archaeologists have identified a plethora of beads fashioned from Tritia gibbosula shells in many Middle Stone Age (MSA) sites across western North Africa, including El Mnasra Cave, Morocco. In a 2021 paper, El Mehdi Sehasseh et al. attribute these artifacts’ apparent ______ to the evolution and propagation of symbolic behavior (e.g., use of personal ornaments) in humans and the widespread availability of T. gibbosula during the MSA.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Archaeologists have identified a plethora of beads fashioned from Tritia gibbosula shells in many Middle Stone Age (MSA) sites across western","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"North Africa, including El Mnasra Cave, Morocco. In a 2021 paper, El Mehdi Sehasseh et al. attribute these artifacts’ apparent ______ to the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"evolution and propagation of symbolic behavior (e.g., use of personal ornaments) in humans and the widespread availability of T. gibbosula","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"during the MSA.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["heterogeneity","ubiquity","expediency","fecundity"]},{"id":"d3104a68","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"Birds sing to communicate over potentially great distances. For this reason, many researchers believe that birds in densely vegetated habitats generally sing at lower frequencies than birds living in comparatively sparse habitats, since dense vegetation tends to ______ the distance that high-frequency sounds can travel.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Birds sing to communicate over potentially great distances. For this reason, many researchers believe that birds in densely vegetated habitats","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"generally sing at lower frequencies than birds living in comparatively sparse habitats, since dense vegetation tends to ______ the distance that","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"high-frequency sounds can travel.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["exceed","diminish","encompass","conceal"]},{"id":"3f753a8e","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"Investigating whether shared false visual memories —specific but inaccurate and widely held recollections of images such as product logos — are caused by people’s previous ______ incorrect renditions of the images, researchers Deepasri Prasad and Wilma Bainbridge found that, in fact, such memories are often not explained by familiarity with erroneous versions of the images.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Investigating whether shared false visual memories—specific but inaccurate and widely held recollections of images such as product logos—are","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"caused by people’s previous ______ incorrect renditions of the images, researchers Deepasri Prasad and Wilma Bainbridge found that, in fact,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"such memories are often not explained by familiarity with erroneous versions of the images.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["compliance with","exposure to","criteria for","forfeiture of"]},{"id":"53542eb5","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"Shoppers can help keep money cycling within a community by making purchases at small local businesses instead of large retailers. Some cities are ______ programs to encourage this behavior, establishing reward points and other incentives for shopping at small businesses.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Shoppers can help keep money cycling within a community by making purchases at small local businesses instead of large retailers. Some cities","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"are ______ programs to encourage this behavior, establishing reward points and other incentives for shopping at small businesses.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["instituting","occupying","underestimating","encountering"]},{"id":"e26d23c4","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"Proposals to raise the age at which retirees begin receiving government transfers of funds are generally discussed in terms of the effects on transfer recipients, but Andria Smythe has argued that delaying such transfers could ______ wealth creation among working adults by lengthening the period in which they are providing financial support to their nonworking parents.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Proposals to raise the age at which retirees begin receiving government transfers of funds are generally discussed in terms of the effects on","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"transfer recipients, but Andria Smythe has argued that delaying such transfers could ______ wealth creation among working adults by lengthening","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"the period in which they are providing financial support to their nonworking parents.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["stymie","compound","disparage","outstrip"]},{"id":"98364791","domain":"Craft and Structure","skill":"Words in Context","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Craft and Structure · Words in Context","passage":"In studying the use of external stimuli to reduce the itching sensation caused by an allergic histamine response, Louise Ward and colleagues found that while harmless applications of vibration or warming can provide a temporary distraction, such ______ stimuli actually offer less relief than a stimulus that seems less benign, like a mild electric shock.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"In studying the use of external stimuli to reduce the itching sensation caused by an allergic histamine response, Louise Ward and colleagues","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"found that while harmless applications of vibration or warming can provide a temporary distraction, such ______ stimuli actually offer less relief","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"than a stimulus that seems less benign, like a mild electric shock.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical and precise word or phrase?","choices":["deceptive","innocuous","novel","impractical"]},{"id":"4e9afd7a","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Inferences","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Information and Ideas · Inferences","passage":"The Indus River valley civilization flourished in South Asia from 3300 BCE to 1300 BCE. Many examples of the civilization’s writing system exist, but researchers haven’t yet deciphered it or identified which ancient language it represents. Nevertheless, archaeologists have found historical artifacts, such as clay figures and jewelry, that provide information about the civilization’s customs and how its communities were organized. The archaeologists’ findings therefore suggest that ______","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The Indus River valley civilization flourished in South Asia from 3300 BCE to 1300 BCE. Many examples of the civilization’s writing system exist,","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"but researchers haven’t yet deciphered it or identified which ancient language it represents. Nevertheless, archaeologists have found historical","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"artifacts, such as clay figures and jewelry, that provide information about the civilization’s customs and how its communities were organized. The","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"archaeologists’ findings therefore suggest that ______","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice most logically completes the text?","choices":["investigating an ancient civilization is easier without knowledge of the civilization’s language.","knowing an ancient civilization’s language isn’t necessary in order to learn details about the civilization.","archaeological research should focus on finding additional artifacts rather than deciphering ancient languages.","examining the civilization’s historical artifacts has resolved the debate about this civilization’s language."]},{"id":"0240d11c","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Information and Ideas · Command of Evidence","passage":"In the twentieth century, ethnographers made a concerted effort to collect Mexican American folklore, but they did not always agree about that folklore’s origins. Scholars such as Aurelio Espinosa claimed that Mexican American folklore derived largely from the folklore of Spain, which ruled Mexico and what is now the southwestern United States from the sixteenth to early nineteenth centuries. Scholars such as Américo Paredes, by contrast, argued that while some Spanish influence is undeniable, Mexican American folklore is mainly the product of the ongoing interactions of various cultures in Mexico and the United States.","question":"Which finding, if true, would most directly support Paredes’s argument?","choices":["The folklore that the ethnographers collected included several songs written in the form of a décima, a type of poem originating in late sixteenth-century Spain.","Much of the folklore that the ethnographers collected had similar elements from region to region.","Most of the folklore that the ethnographers collected was previously unknown to scholars.","Most of the folklore that the ethnographers collected consisted of corridos—ballads about history and social life—of a clearly recent origin."]},{"id":"2592e0de","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Information and Ideas · Central Ideas and Details","passage":"Bicycles were first mass-produced in the late nineteenth century throughout Europe and North America, allowing individuals remarkable freedom to travel longer distances quickly and comfortably. This freedom, coupled with the affordability of the vehicle, made the bicycle immensely popular. Individuals were able to live farther from their workplaces, easily visit neighboring towns, and participate in new leisure and sport activities. Bicycling quickly became a popular social endeavor, with enthusiasts forming local cycling clubs to enjoy these newfound activities with others.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Bicycles were first mass-produced in the late nineteenth century throughout Europe and North America, allowing individuals remarkable freedom","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"to travel longer distances quickly and comfortably. This freedom, coupled with the affordability of the vehicle, made the bicycle immensely","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"popular. Individuals were able to live farther from their workplaces, easily visit neighboring towns, and participate in new leisure and sport","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"activities. Bicycling quickly became a popular social endeavor, with enthusiasts forming local cycling clubs to enjoy these newfound activities","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"with others.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice best states the main idea of the text?","choices":["The widespread adoption of the bicycle in the late nineteenth century provided new opportunities for people.","The affordability of the bicycle in the late nineteenth century made it the preferred way to travel.","The popularity of the bicycle in the late nineteenth century gave rise to the first cycling clubs.","The mass production of the bicycle in the late nineteenth century made it safer for people to use."]},{"id":"303537cf","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Central Ideas and Details","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Information and Ideas · Central Ideas and Details","passage":"The following text is adapted from Lewis Carroll’s 1889 satirical novel Sylvie and Bruno. A crowd has gathered outside a room belonging to the Warden, an official who reports to the Lord Chancellor. One man, who was more excited than the rest, flung his hat high into the air, and shouted (as well as I could make out) “Who roar for the Sub- Warden?” Everybody roared, but whether it was for the Sub-Warden, or not, did not clearly appear: some were shouting “Bread!” and some Taxes! , but no one seemed to know what it was they really wanted. All this I saw from the open window of the Warden’s breakfast-saloon, looking across the shoulder of the Lord Chancellor. “","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"The following text is adapted from Lewis Carroll’s 1889 satirical novel Sylvie and Bruno. A crowd has gathered outside a room belonging to the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Warden, an official who reports to the Lord Chancellor.","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"One man, who was more excited than the rest, flung his hat high into the air, and shouted (as well as I could make out) “Who roar for the Sub-","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Warden?” Everybody roared, but whether it was for the Sub-Warden, or not, did not clearly appear: some were shouting “Bread!” and some","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"“Taxes!”, but no one seemed to know what it was they really wanted.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"All this I saw from the open window of the Warden’s breakfast-saloon, looking across the shoulder of the Lord Chancellor.","indent":0},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"“What can it all mean?” he kept repeating to himself. “I never heard such shouting before—and at this time of the morning, too! And with such","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"unanimity!”","indent":0}],"question":"What can it all mean?","choices":["He asks about the meaning of the crowd’s shouting, even though he claims to know what the crowd wants.","He indicates a desire to speak to the crowd, even though the crowd has asked to speak to the Sub-Warden.","He expresses sympathy for the crowd’s demands, even though the crowd’s shouting annoys him.","He describes the crowd as being united, even though the crowd clearly appears otherwise."]},{"id":"b074cc05","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Information and Ideas · Command of Evidence","passage":"Employment in Technology in Hawaii in 2010 and 2019 7 Approximate number of jobs 6 5 (in thousands) 4 3 2 1 0 2010 2019 Year engineering services technical consulting services computer services A student in Hawaii is interested in pursuing a career in technology and decides to do some research on local trends. The student notices that the number of jobs in computer services in 2010 was ______","passage_lines":[{"type":"chart","note":"[Graph: Employment in Technology in Hawaii in 2010 and 2019 — not available here]"},{"type":"text","text":"A student in Hawaii is interested in pursuing a career in technology and decides to do some research on local trends. The student notices that the number of jobs in computer services in 2010 was ______"}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses data from the graph to complete the statement?","choices":["higher than the number of jobs in technical consulting services, and in 2019 was about the same as the number of jobs in engineering services.","about the same as the number of jobs in engineering services, and in 2019 was about the same as the number of jobs in technical consulting services.","lower than the number of jobs in engineering services, but in 2019 was higher than the number of jobs in engineering services.","about the same as the number of jobs in technical consulting services, but in 2019 was lower than the number of jobs in technical consulting services."],"unusable":"missing graph"},{"id":"e7dc27dc","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Information and Ideas · Command of Evidence","passage":"As a monthly newsletter formed in 1969 by a group of Asian American students at the University of California, Los Angeles, Gidra helped raise awareness about social and political issues concerning the Asian American community on campus and at large. The newsletter had an expansive reach for a publication of its kind: around 4,000 copies were published each month. A student writing a history paper, however, hypothesizes that Gidra’s influence cannot be measured by the number of newsletters published monthly alone.","question":"Which finding, if true, would most directly support the student’s hypothesis?","choices":["The students who initially formed Gidra each contributed financially to its creation.","In addition to covering current events, Gidra also featured works of art and literature.","Gidra was initially based out of the Asian American Studies Center at UCLA.","People would often give their copies of Gidra to others once they had finished reading an issue."]},{"id":"ccf414c9","domain":"Information and Ideas","skill":"Command of Evidence","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Information and Ideas · Command of Evidence","passage":"E-book Sales as a Percentage of Total Unit Sales in All Book Formats for a Large US Trade Publisher, by Genre, 2006, 2011, 2016 Genre 2006 2011 2016 science fiction and fantasy 0.6 27.7 36.7 cookbooks 0 2.9 10.5 travel guides 0 5.5 24.6 romance 0.3 40.6 56.2 E-books became an increasingly popular means of reading in the United States in the 2000s and 2010s, though that popularity was concentrated in titles that, like those in most fiction genres, are meant to be read straight through from beginning to end. For books in nonfiction genres that do not tell stories and require the reader to flip back and forth through a volume, e-books were significantly less commercially successful. This can be seen by comparing ______","passage_lines":[{"type":"table","title":"E-book Sales as a Percentage of Total Unit Sales in All Book Formats for a Large US Trade Publisher, by Genre, 2006, 2011, 2016","headers":["Genre","2006","2011","2016"],"rows":[["science fiction and fantasy","0.6","27.7","36.7"],["cookbooks","0","2.9","10.5"],["travel guides","0","5.5","24.6"],["romance","0.3","40.6","56.2"]]},{"type":"text","text":"E-books became an increasingly popular means of reading in the United States in the 2000s and 2010s, though that popularity was concentrated in titles that, like those in most fiction genres, are meant to be read straight through from beginning to end. For books in nonfiction genres that do not tell stories and require the reader to flip back and forth through a volume, e-books were significantly less commercially successful. This can be seen by comparing ______"}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses data from the table to illustrate the claim?","choices":["the percentage of 2016 cookbook sales that were e-books with the percentage of 2016 science fiction and fantasy sales that were e-books.","the percentage of 2006 romance sales that were e-books with the percentage of 2016 romance sales that were e-books.","the percentage of 2006 romance sales that were e-books with the 2006 science fiction and fantasy sales that were e-books.","the percentage of 2011 travel guide sales that were e-books with the percentage of 2016 travel guide sales that were e-books."]},{"id":"dbd78791","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Standard English · Form, Structure, and Sense","passage":"Led by Syrian American astronomer Shadia Habbal, the Solar Wind Sherpas are an intrepid team of scientists who travel the globe to study solar winds, the streams of particles emanating from the Sun that are only visible from certain locations during a total solar eclipse. When such an eclipse is imminent, the Sherpas pack up their telescopes and ______ ready.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"Led by Syrian American astronomer Shadia Habbal, the Solar Wind Sherpas are an intrepid team of scientists who travel the globe to study solar","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"winds, the streams of particles emanating from the Sun that are only visible from certain locations during a total solar eclipse. When such an","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"eclipse is imminent, the Sherpas pack up their telescopes and ______ ready.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["get","had gotten","got","were getting"]},{"id":"9f737b2a","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Standard English · Form, Structure, and Sense","passage":"In Death Valley National Park’s Racetrack Playa, a flat, dry lakebed, are 162 rocks — some weighing less than a pound but others almost 700 pounds — that move periodically from place to place, seemingly of their own volition. Racetrack-like trails in the ______ mysterious migration.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"In Death Valley National Park’s Racetrack Playa, a flat, dry lakebed, are 162 rocks—some weighing less than a pound but others almost 700","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"pounds—that move periodically from place to place, seemingly of their own volition. Racetrack-like trails in the ______ mysterious migration.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["playas sediment mark the rock’s","playa’s sediment mark the rocks","playa’s sediment mark the rocks’","playas’ sediment mark the rocks’"]},{"id":"155239cf","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Boundaries","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Standard English · Boundaries","passage":"By analyzing ice cores from Greenland and Antarctica, a research team at Sweden’s Lund University discovered evidence of a solar storm that occurred 9,200 years ago. Scientists had previously thought the Sun to be in a relatively “quiet” phase at that ______ the Lund team’s finding suggests otherwise.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"By analyzing ice cores from Greenland and Antarctica, a research team at Sweden’s Lund University discovered evidence of a solar storm that","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"occurred 9,200 years ago. Scientists had previously thought the Sun to be in a relatively “quiet” phase at that ______ the Lund team’s finding","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"suggests otherwise.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["time but","time, but","time,","time"]},{"id":"819c443d","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Standard English · Form, Structure, and Sense","passage":"In January 1776, Thomas Paine published Common Sense, an appeal for freedom from the British monarchy that famously helped ignite the desire for independence among the American colonists. After the colonies achieved their independence, Paine moved to Paris, where the provocative ______ would contribute to another revolution —the French Revolution.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"In January 1776, Thomas Paine published Common Sense, an appeal for freedom from the British monarchy that famously helped ignite the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"desire for independence among the American colonists. After the colonies achieved their independence, Paine moved to Paris, where the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"provocative ______ would contribute to another revolution—the French Revolution.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["authors political writings","author’s political writings","author’s political writing’s","authors’ political writings’"]},{"id":"975eda7c","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Standard English · Form, Structure, and Sense","passage":"For thousands of years, people in the Americas ______ the bottle gourd, a large bitter fruit with a thick rind, to make bottles, other types of containers, and even musical instruments. Oddly, there is no evidence that any type of bottle gourd is native to the Western Hemisphere; either the fruit or its seeds must have somehow been carried from Asia or Africa.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"For thousands of years, people in the Americas ______ the bottle gourd, a large bitter fruit with a thick rind, to make bottles, other types of","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"containers, and even musical instruments. Oddly, there is no evidence that any type of bottle gourd is native to the Western Hemisphere; either","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"the fruit or its seeds must have somehow been carried from Asia or Africa.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["to use","have used","having used","using"]},{"id":"eee7536f","domain":"Standard English","skill":"Form, Structure, and Sense","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Standard English · Form, Structure, and Sense","passage":"On March 27, 2004, after nine years as a professional boxer, Isra Girgrah retired. The Yemeni American athlete ______ her career with a record of twenty-eight wins, three losses, and two ties.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"On March 27, 2004, after nine years as a professional boxer, Isra Girgrah retired. The Yemeni American athlete ______ her career with a record of","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"twenty-eight wins, three losses, and two ties.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text so that it conforms to the conventions of Standard English?","choices":["finish","has been finishing","finished","will be finishing"]},{"id":"de01ccef","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: The background colors of US and UK road signs are used to denote each sign’s purpose. Directional signs are a type of sign containing information such as route names, distance to a destination, etc. Highways, major roadways, and minor roadways in the US generally use green for directional signs. Highways in the UK generally use blue for directional signs. Major roadways in the UK generally use green for directional signs. The student wants to contrast how green backgrounds are used in US and UK road signs.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"The background colors of US and UK road signs are used to denote each sign’s purpose.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Directional signs are a type of sign containing information such as route names, distance to a destination, etc.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"Highways, major roadways, and minor roadways in the US generally use green for directional signs.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Highways in the UK generally use blue for directional signs.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Major roadways in the UK generally use green for directional signs.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Blue backgrounds are generally used on directional highway signs in the UK, in contrast to the green highway signs used in the US.","In the US, green signs indicate directional information on both highways and major roadways, but in the UK, directional signs of this color generally appear only on major roadways.","Green road signs are used in both the US and UK to denote directional information on major roadways, like distance to a destination or route names.","Both the UK and the US use directional signs, which include information on route names and distance to a destination."]},{"id":"a204d618","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Expression of Ideas · Transitions","passage":"In 1949, Frank Zamboni developed an ice rink resurfacing machine. As Zamboni’s machine moved along the rink’s surface, it first scraped off the top layer of ice. ______ it sprayed water into the deep grooves left behind by customers skates. Lastly, it smoothed over the newly formed ice.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"In 1949, Frank Zamboni developed an ice rink resurfacing machine. As Zamboni’s machine moved along the rink’s surface, it first scraped off the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"top layer of ice. ______ it sprayed water into the deep grooves left behind by customers’ skates. Lastly, it smoothed over the newly formed ice.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["For example,","Next,","Similarly,","In contrast,"]},{"id":"07456405","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Hard","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: Malapportionment is the over- or underrepresentation (relative to population size) of electoral districts in a governing body. It is a common feature of representative governments. There are 169 seats in Norway’s supreme legislature (the Storting). Seats are distributed by a formula that awards 1 point per resident and 1.8 points per unit of land. Less populated rural districts with large tracts of land receive a disproportionate number of seats compared to smaller but more populated urban districts. The student wants to refute a claim that malapportionment in the Storting favors small urban districts.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Malapportionment is the over- or underrepresentation (relative to population size) of electoral districts in a governing body.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"It is a common feature of representative governments.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"There are 169 seats in Norway’s supreme legislature (the Storting).","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Seats are distributed by a formula that awards 1 point per resident and 1.8 points per unit of land.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Less populated rural districts with large tracts of land receive a disproportionate number of seats compared to smaller but more","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"populated urban districts.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["Less populated rural districts are disproportionally underrepresented in the Storting, creating an unfair advantage for smaller but more populated urban districts.","It’s untrue that malapportionment in the 169-seat Storting favors small urban districts; rather, the formula for distributing seats overrepresents more populated districts.","A common feature of representative governments, malapportionment occurs when electoral districts are over- or underrepresented.","Awarding more points per unit of land than points per resident, the formula for distributing Storting seats overrepresents less populated rural districts with large tracts of land."]},{"id":"ff8d2125","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Rhetorical Synthesis","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Expression of Ideas · Rhetorical Synthesis","passage":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes: Crown shyness is a phenomenon in which the tops (crowns) of neighboring trees grow close together but don’t overlap. To explain how this happens, Australian forester M.R. Jacobs proposes the mutual abrasion theory. According to Jacobs’s theory, when trees brush against one another, branches break off. Malaysian scholar Francis S.P. Ng posits the mutual shade avoidance theory. According to Ng’s theory, when tree branches detect shade from nearby trees’ branches, they stop growing. The student wants to compare the causes of crown shyness proposed in the two theories.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"While researching a topic, a student has taken the following notes:","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"Crown shyness is a phenomenon in which the tops (crowns) of neighboring trees grow close together but don’t overlap.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"To explain how this happens, Australian forester M.R. Jacobs proposes the mutual abrasion theory.","indent":2},{"type":"blank"},{"type":"line","text":"According to Jacobs’s theory, when trees brush against one another, branches break off.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"Malaysian scholar Francis S.P. Ng posits the mutual shade avoidance theory.","indent":2},{"type":"line","text":"According to Ng’s theory, when tree branches detect shade from nearby trees’ branches, they stop growing.","indent":2}],"question":"Which choice most effectively uses relevant information from the notes to accomplish this goal?","choices":["While Jacobs proposes that crown shyness is caused by neighboring tree branches brushing against one another, Ng posits that it occurs when branches detect shade from nearby trees’ branches.","Both Jacobs and Ng have proposed theories to explain what causes crown shyness.","Ng posits the mutual shade avoidance theory, whereas Jacobs proposes an alternative theory.","Jacobs’s mutual abrasion theory proposes that when neighboring trees brush against one another, branches break off, resulting in a phenomenon in which the tops of trees grow close together but don’t overlap."]},{"id":"37ec26e7","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"Easy","context":"Expression of Ideas · Transitions","passage":"In order to create the Global Positioning System (GPS), scientists had to develop an accurate mathematical model of Earth’s shape that accounted for various forces, such as tides. ______ it was mathematician Gladys West who wrote the computer program that could perform these necessary calculations.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"In order to create the Global Positioning System (GPS), scientists had to develop an accurate mathematical model of Earth’s shape that","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"accounted for various forces, such as tides. ______ it was mathematician Gladys West who wrote the computer program that could perform","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"these necessary calculations.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["Ultimately,","In other words,","Secondly,","In addition,"]},{"id":"4fde4454","domain":"Expression of Ideas","skill":"Transitions","difficulty":"Medium","context":"Expression of Ideas · Transitions","passage":"One poll taken after the first 1960 presidential debate suggested that John Kennedy lost badly: only 21 percent of those who listened on the radio rated him the winner. ______ the debate was ultimately considered a victory for the telegenic young senator, who rated higher than his opponent, Vice President Richard Nixon, among those watching on the new medium of television.","passage_lines":[{"type":"line","text":"One poll taken after the first 1960 presidential debate suggested that John Kennedy lost badly: only 21 percent of those who listened on the","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"radio rated him the winner. ______ the debate was ultimately considered a victory for the telegenic young senator, who rated higher than his","indent":0},{"type":"line","text":"opponent, Vice President Richard Nixon, among those watching on the new medium of television.","indent":0}],"question":"Which choice completes the text with the most logical transition?","choices":["In other words,","Therefore,","Likewise,","Nevertheless,"]}];
